# enemies design rows: the duel tiers as designer data (Bycer ch8).
# Designers change numbers here without a Rust edit; the AI seat loads
# these rows into tier skill (GA13) and the game test below checks them.
# Whole numbers only, `#` comments, no floats. Units live in the key:
# `_permille` is parts per thousand, `_ticks` is fixed ticks at 60 Hz.
# Columns: hp_permille (narrow 900-1200 band, skill first), tell_ticks
# (readable startup, 30 or more: 500 ms at 60 Hz), stagger_permille (poise
# hold 0-1000, higher holds longer), counter (1 parry, 2 dodge, 3 block: the
# answer from the player's own kit that beats this tier).
# Cards live in docs/design/ENEMIES.md: tier_1 is the Holder grunt
# (teaches parry), tier_2 is the Flanker veteran (teaches dodge),
# tier_3 is the Holder elite (stamina pressure, riposte), tier_4 is the
# primus boss (parry, then dodge in phase 2 at or below 500 hp). One melee
# enemy at a time per encounter; StrikePhase is the shared 30/1/6 clock.
# House numbers: the 60% tier bar (a tier beats the one below in 60% or
# more of seeded duels) and the 2-4 tick human noise stay until the
# tournament and human tapes prove another number.
# tier_4 is the primus boss row: the design card (docs/design/ENEMIES.md)
# answers the owner ask (EB-2026-10-13-S2) and crates/ai/src/depth.rs ships
# the same numbers as `PRIMUS_TIER`; the GA13 tournament hook reads the
# ladder later. The row stays inside the narrow hp band and the 30-tick tell.
table enemies version 1
tier_1 hp_permille=950 tell_ticks=30 stagger_permille=400 counter=1
tier_2 hp_permille=1000 tell_ticks=30 stagger_permille=500 counter=2
tier_3 hp_permille=1050 tell_ticks=30 stagger_permille=600 counter=1
tier_4 hp_permille=1100 tell_ticks=30 stagger_permille=700 counter=1
